  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "The equation of the normal line to the curve $y=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}$ at the point $(1,4)$ is given by $x+py=q$ where $p$ and $q$ are constants. Find the value of $q-p.$",
  "worked_solution": "Let $f(x)=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{-1/2}) \\\\  & =-2{x}^{-3/2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{\u2032}(1)=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{1}^{3}}}=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{\u2032}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{\u2032}=-\\frac{1}{m}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-4 & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-1) \\\\ 2y-8 & =x-1 \\\\ x-2y & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $q-p=-7-(-2)=-5.$",
